// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ---------------- Persians (Sasanian and later Iran) ----------------
  "persians:hunt": "In the great rock arch of Taq-e Bostan, carved around 600 CE, Khosrow II stands in a reed boat while elephants drive wild boar through a marsh toward his bow. Other boats carry women playing harps, and the king's halo marks him as chosen. For the Sasanian court the hunt in the walled royal park, the pardis, was more than sport: a shah who mastered wild beasts showed that he could hold the world in order.",
  "persians:battle": "Near Edessa in 260 CE, Shapur I met the Roman army and took the emperor Valerian himself prisoner. The savaran, horse and rider wrapped in mail, charged behind trumpets and drums, and the victory was cut into the cliff at Naqsh-e Rostam: Shapur mounted, one Roman emperor kneeling before him, another held by the wrist. For Iran it was proof that the kingdom of Ahura Mazda stood equal to Rome.",
  "persians:gather": "At Mehregan, the autumn feast of Mithra, the harvest of the Iranian plateau came in: wheat from fields watered by underground qanats, pomegranates, grapes, saffron picked thread by thread before dawn. Sasanian kings wore crowns shaped like the sun and received the gifts of the season, and people exchanged sweets and promises. Al-Biruni later wrote that after Nowruz it was the greatest feast of the Persians, a thanksgiving for a year that had not failed.",
  "persians:ritual": "Before a great war the Sasanian kings walked on foot, not riding, to Adur Gushnasp, the fire temple of the warriors at Takht-e Soleyman beside its deep blue spring lake. Priests in white veils, mouths covered so their breath would not touch the flame, fed it sandalwood and recited the Avesta. The king brought gifts and asked Ahura Mazda for victory. No one shouted; the fire answered only by burning on.",
  "persians:rain": "In the Avesta the star Tishtrya, Sirius, fights the drought demon Apaosha at the edge of the sea: a white horse with golden ears against a black, bald horse. Three nights Tishtrya loses, because people forgot to pray; when they remember, he wins and the clouds roll over the land. Each summer at Tirgan, Iranian villages splashed water on one another and tied rainbow threads on their wrists, waiting for that victory.",
  "persians:flood": "Around 628 CE, in the last years of Khosrow II, the Tigris and Euphrates rose higher than anyone remembered and broke the dykes of the Sawad, the rich black land of Mesopotamia. Al-Baladhuri records that the king went himself to the breaches and had workers punished when the embankments failed again. They could not be closed; the water stayed for centuries as the Great Swamp, and the empire never recovered its old harvests.",
  "persians:wind": "In Sistan, on Iran's eastern border, the wind of 120 days, bad-e sad-o-bist-ruz, blows every summer without stopping. It buries villages in sand and blinds travellers. People did not only suffer it: at Nashtifan the old vertical windmills of clay and wood, perhaps the oldest design in the world, still turn in it and grind grain. To live there was to make a partner of the storm.",
  "persians:fire": "At Sadeh, fifty days before Nowruz, Iranians lit huge bonfires to celebrate the discovery of fire. The Shahnameh tells how King Hushang threw a stone at a serpent, missed, struck a rock, and saw a spark leap out. He bowed to the light and ordered a feast. Fire was never worshipped as a god, but as the purest sign of Ahura Mazda, and the midwinter flames promised that the cold would end.",
  "persians:thunder": "Imagined: a Zagros shepherd brings his flock down from the high pasture as black clouds pile over the peaks. Lightning cracks across the slopes, and he counts the seconds to the thunder, praying the storm will pass to the next valley. His grandmother says the sky is fighting the demons of drought, and that every peal is good news for the wheat below, even if it frightens the sheep.",
  "persians:birth": "The Shahnameh says Rudabeh's labour with Rostam was so hard she nearly died. Zal burned a feather of the Simurgh, the great bird appeared, and told him to give her wine, open her side with a blade, lift the child out, and heal the wound with herbs and a stroke of the feather. Persians still call a caesarean birth rostamzad. The baby, huge and crying, would one day save Iran again and again.",
  "persians:death": "A Zoroastrian funeral was quiet and careful. The body was washed, dressed in white, and shown to a dog, the sagdid, whose gaze was believed to drive away corruption. Bearers carried it in pairs to the dakhma, the tower of silence, where sun and birds would return it to nature without defiling earth or fire. Mourners followed holding a cloth between them, and priests recited the Avesta; on the fourth dawn the soul crossed the Chinvat Bridge.",

  // ---------------- Achaemenids ----------------
  "achaemenids:hunt": "On his cylinder seal, now in the British Museum, Darius the Great stands in a chariot under the winged disc of Ahura Mazda, drawing his bow at a rearing lion. Persian kings hunted in vast walled parks the Greeks called paradeisoi; Xenophon describes Cyrus the Younger riding down wild beasts at Celaenae to train himself and his men. The hunt was the king's proof of courage, and the court's school of war.",
  "achaemenids:battle": "At Thermopylae in August 480 BCE, Xerxes waited four days for the small Greek army to withdraw. When the Medes and then his own Immortals were thrown back in the narrow pass, Herodotus says the king leapt three times from his throne in fear for his men. Then Ephialtes showed the mountain path, the Immortals marched through the night, and the pass fell. Victory came, but bitter and costly.",
  "achaemenids:gather": "On the staircase of the Apadana at Persepolis, twenty-three peoples climb toward the king in stone: Lydians with gold bowls, Bactrians leading a two-humped camel, Ethiopians carrying an elephant tusk, Indians with spice jars on a yoke. Each delegation is led by the hand, peacefully. Darius wanted the gathering of the empire's harvests to look like a festival of many nations, not a list of the conquered.",
  "achaemenids:ritual": "Herodotus says that at sunrise before crossing the Hellespont in 480 BCE, Xerxes poured wine from a golden cup into the sea, praying to the sun that nothing would stop him before he reached the ends of Europe. Then he threw the cup, a golden bowl and a Persian sword into the water. Myrtle branches were spread on the bridges, incense burned, and the army waited silently for the gods' answer.",
  "achaemenids:rain": "Imagined: in a dry year in Parsa, villagers stand at their irrigation channels watching the mountains for cloud. In the city, a clerk notes grain rations on clay tablets that will survive for 2,500 years. Carved over the terrace of Persepolis is Darius's own prayer: may Ahura Mazda protect this land from the enemy army, from famine, and from the Lie. Everyone hopes the second wish is heard first.",
  "achaemenids:flood": "Herodotus tells that when Cyrus marched on Babylon, one of his sacred white horses was swept away and drowned in the Gyndes river. Furious, Cyrus spent a whole summer having his army dig 360 channels until the river could be crossed by a woman without wetting her knees. Then, in 539 BCE, he diverted the Euphrates itself and his men walked into Babylon along the riverbed.",
  "achaemenids:wind": "In 524 BCE, Cambyses sent fifty thousand men from Thebes across the Western Desert to the oracle of Amun at Siwa. Herodotus says that half-way, as they ate their midday meal, a great south wind rose and buried them in sand. Not one returned. The Egyptians took it as the god's answer; for Persian families it was simply an army that vanished, and mothers who waited for sons who never came.",
  "achaemenids:fire": "In 330 BCE Alexander set fire to the palaces of Persepolis. Ancient writers say it happened at a drunken feast; others say it was revenge for the burning of Athens. Cedar roof beams brought from Lebanon fell blazing into the Hall of a Hundred Columns, and the archive of clay tablets was baked hard by the heat. For Persians it was the night the house of Darius ended.",
  "achaemenids:thunder": "In 480 BCE a Persian detachment marched on Delphi to take its treasures. Herodotus, telling the Greek side, says that as they reached the temple of Athena Pronaia, thunderbolts fell from the sky, two crags broke from Parnassus and crushed many, and a war cry was heard from inside the shrine. The survivors fled. Whatever truly happened, the soldiers knew mountain storms could be stronger than any king.",
  "achaemenids:birth": "Herodotus tells that King Astyages of the Medes dreamed his grandson would overthrow him and ordered the newborn killed. His steward Harpagus gave the baby to a herdsman, Mitradates, whose wife Spako had just borne a stillborn son. She begged to keep the living child and expose the dead one instead. The boy raised among the cattle was Cyrus, who would found the greatest empire the world had seen.",
  "achaemenids:death": "Cyrus the Great was laid in a gold coffin in a small stone house on a stepped plinth at Pasargadae, around 530 BCE. Arrian says Magi guarded it, received a sheep daily and a horse each month to sacrifice for him. Plutarch records the inscription: O man, I am Cyrus, who won the Persians their empire; do not grudge me this little earth that covers my body. Alexander, finding it robbed, was moved.",

  // ---------------- Saracens (early Arab and medieval Arab peoples) ----------------
  "saracens:hunt": "Usama ibn Munqidh, the 12th-century emir of Shayzar on the Orontes, wrote fondly of his father's hunts: falcons and sakers on the wrist, cheetahs riding behind the saddle, salukis running down gazelle and hare on the plain. His father knew each hawk by name and wrote down every kill. In old age, Usama remembered those mornings as the happiest days of a life full of wars.",
  "saracens:battle": "In August 636, on the Yarmouk river, the Arab armies under Khalid ibn al-Walid faced the Byzantine host for six days in heat and dust. When the Arab lines bent back toward the camp, the women there, among them Hind bint Utbah, struck the retreating men with tent poles and drove them back to fight. On the last day the Byzantines were trapped in the ravine of Wadi al-Raqqad, and Syria passed to Islam.",
  "saracens:gather": "In late summer the date harvest came to the oases of Medina and al-Ahsa. Climbers went up the palms with a rope belt, cut the heavy clusters and lowered them to people waiting below, while children gathered fallen fruit. The dates were dried and pressed into skins to feed caravans through the winter. In a land with little else, a good harvest meant guests could be fed and no one need go hungry.",
  "saracens:ritual": "Before a journey, a marriage or a campaign, a Muslim does not consult omens: he prays istikhara. Two units of prayer, then the supplication the Prophet taught: O God, I ask You to choose for me by Your knowledge; if this matter is good for my faith, my life and my end, make it easy for me, and if it is bad, turn it away from me. Then he sleeps, and accepts what comes.",
  "saracens:rain": "In 638, the Year of Ashes, drought and hunger struck Arabia so hard that dust storms turned the earth grey. The caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab went out to pray the istisqa, the prayer for rain, holding the hand of al-Abbas, the Prophet's elderly uncle, and wept as he asked forgiveness. The reports say the clouds gathered before they left the place, and the people ran home through the first rain in months.",
  "saracens:wind": "In 627, during the Battle of the Trench, ten thousand confederates besieged Medina for weeks. Then on a winter night a fierce, freezing wind tore through their camp, overturning cooking pots, blowing out fires and scattering tents. Abu Sufyan, their leader, mounted his camel and ordered the retreat. The Quran recalls it: We sent against them a wind and hosts you could not see. In Medina, the wind was mercy.",
  "saracens:fire": "On the hills of pre-Islamic Arabia, generous men lit nar al-qira, the fire of hospitality, so that lost travellers in the night would see it and come to eat. Hatim al-Ta'i, remembered for centuries as the most generous of the Arabs, is said to have told his slave: light the fire high, and if it brings a guest, you are free. In the desert, a fire on a hill meant life.",
  "saracens:thunder": "The Quran says: the thunder glorifies His praise, and the angels also, in awe of Him. Desert Arabs watched far-off lightning, barq, with longing, because it meant rain was falling somewhere. Imru' al-Qais ended his Mu'allaqa watching such a storm at night, its lightning like a lamp in a monk's hand, the rain uprooting trees and pouring over the hills of Qatan until the valleys ran.",
  "saracens:birth": "When a Muslim child is born, the father whispers the call to prayer into the right ear, so that the first words the baby hears are God is greatest. Someone pious chews a date and rubs it on the infant's palate, the tahnik. On the seventh day the hair is shaved, its weight in silver is given to the poor, and a sheep is sacrificed for the aqiqah. No one knows what this child will become; everyone prays it will be good.",
  "saracens:death": "An Islamic funeral has no music. The body is washed an odd number of times, wrapped in plain white cloth, and carried quickly, the same day if possible, by men who take turns at the bier. The prayer for the dead is said standing, without bowing. In the grave the body lies on its right side, facing Mecca. Loud wailing is discouraged; quiet tears are not. Khalid ibn al-Walid, scarred by a hundred battles, died in bed at Homs and wept that he had not died a martyr.",

  // ---------------- Abbasid Dynasty ----------------
  "abbasids:hunt": "The Abbasid caliphs at Samarra kept great walled game reserves, the hayr, stocked with gazelle, onager and lions. They rode out with cheetahs on cushions behind the saddle, falcons on gloved fists and long-legged salukis. Abu Nuwas, the wittiest poet of Baghdad, invented a whole genre for it, the tardiyyat, hunting poems that praise a hound's speed and a falcon's eye as lovingly as wine.",
  "abbasids:battle": "In 837 Byzantine raiders sacked the frontier town of Zapetra, and a captive Muslim woman was said to have cried out: O Mu'tasim! When the caliph heard, he swore to answer. Astrologers warned the stars were against a campaign, but in August 838 al-Mu'tasim took Amorium, the Byzantine emperor's ancestral city. Abu Tammam celebrated it in a famous ode: the sword is truer than the books.",
  "abbasids:gather": "Around Baghdad lay the Sawad, the black land between the rivers, crossed by canals. Under the Abbasids farmers grew wheat and barley, dates, rice, sugar cane, cotton and lemons brought from India and Persia. At harvest the tax-collectors measured the kharaj, and barges carried grain down the canals to the Round City. Ibn Wahshiyya's Nabataean Agriculture recorded their know-how, proud that the old soil still fed an empire.",
  "abbasids:ritual": "When al-Mansur chose the site of Baghdad by the Tigris, he did not begin the work lightly. The court astrologers Nawbakht and Masha'allah calculated the moment, and on 30 July 762 the first brick was laid with a prayer. The caliph traced the walls of the Round City in lines of ash and set them alight at night so he could see its plan. He named it Madinat al-Salam, the City of Peace.",
  "abbasids:rain": "A famous story says Harun al-Rashid once looked up at a passing cloud and said to it: rain wherever you wish, your harvest will come back to me. It was a boast about the reach of the caliphate, from North Africa to Central Asia. But farmers along the Tigris knew no caliph commanded the sky, and in dry winters they gathered in the mosques for the prayer for rain like everyone else.",
  "abbasids:flood": "Baghdad lived between two rivers and paid for it. Chroniclers such as Ibn al-Jawzi recorded year after year when the Tigris rose in spring, broke its banks and poured into the city, collapsing mud-brick houses, flooding cemeteries and turning markets into lakes. People carried their families onto rooftops and prayed. Each time, when the water fell, they rebuilt on the same ground, because it was still Baghdad.",
  "abbasids:wind": "Imagined: on a summer afternoon a dust storm rolls in from the western desert. The sky over Baghdad turns yellow, then red, then dark as night. Merchants pull shutters across their shops on the Karkh market, boatmen tie up on the Tigris, and scholars in the House of Wisdom cover their manuscripts with cloth. When it passes, everything is coated in fine dust, and the muezzin calls as if nothing happened.",
  "abbasids:fire": "In February 1258 the Mongol army of Hulagu broke into Baghdad. For days the city burned. The caliph al-Musta'sim was killed, and the great libraries, including the House of Wisdom, were destroyed; chroniclers said so many books were thrown into the Tigris that its water ran black with ink, then red with blood. For Muslims everywhere it was the end of a world that had lasted five hundred years.",
  "abbasids:thunder": "Imagined: a winter storm breaks over Baghdad. Thunder rolls across the domes and the children of a scholar's house run to him in fear. He tells them what al-Kindi wrote, that thunder and lightning come from vapours colliding in the clouds, and then adds the verse every child knows: the thunder glorifies His praise. Science and prayer sit side by side, and the children fall asleep to the rain.",
  "abbasids:birth": "In September 786, on a single night, the caliph al-Hadi died, his brother Harun al-Rashid became caliph, and Harun's son Abdallah, later al-Ma'mun, was born. Baghdad called it the night of three caliphs. A child born that night seemed blessed beyond doubt. Yet years later he would fight a civil war against his half-brother al-Amin, and also found the greatest age of science in Islam.",
  "abbasids:death": "In 809 Harun al-Rashid fell ill on campaign near Tus in Khorasan. Knowing he would die, he had his grave dug in a garden there and looked down into it, saying: son of Adam, this is your end. He was washed, shrouded and prayed over by his son Salih, and buried quickly, as Muslims are, without music. The caliph of the Thousand and One Nights lies in a simple grave far from Baghdad.",

  // ---------------- Ayyubids ----------------
  "ayyubids:hunt": "Imagined: in the green Ghouta outside Damascus, a young Ayyubid prince rides out at dawn with his falconers. A saker leaves the glove, climbs, and stoops on a crane over the Barada river. The old huntsman praises the bird, not the prince. They return at noon with cranes and hares for the evening meal, and the prince's uncle reminds him that hunting is only practice for the war with the Franks.",
  "ayyubids:battle": "On 4 July 1187, at the Horns of Hattin above the Sea of Galilee, Saladin trapped the crusader army without water. His men set the dry grass alight so smoke blew into the thirsty knights' faces. The True Cross was captured and King Guy surrendered. In his tent Saladin handed Guy a cup of iced rose-water, and when Guy passed it on to Reynald of Chatillon, Saladin said: I did not give it to him.",
  "ayyubids:gather": "In the Ghouta, the oasis of orchards around Damascus, the apricot harvest came in early summer. Families climbed into the trees, and the fruit was dried in sheets on rooftops into qamar al-din, apricot leather, to be sold across the Ayyubid lands. In Egypt the sugar cane was cut and pressed in sultans' mills. Under Saladin's heirs, peace with the Franks meant harvests and trade could flourish.",
  "ayyubids:ritual": "Baha al-Din ibn Shaddad, Saladin's judge and biographer, writes that the sultan liked to begin battles on a Friday, at the hour of the Friday prayer, so that preachers in every mosque would be praying for the Muslim armies at the very moment they met the enemy. He prayed himself before every engagement, and never consulted astrologers. The answer, he believed, belonged to God alone.",
  "ayyubids:rain": "Imagined: a dry winter in Damascus, the Barada river low between its banks. The people go out to the musalla outside the city, dressed in old clothes, humble, the old and the children with them. The imam leads the prayer for rain and turns his cloak inside out, as the Prophet did, hoping the sky will turn too. On the walk home, a wind brings the smell of wet dust.",
  "ayyubids:flood": "In 1221 the army of the Fifth Crusade marched up the Nile from Damietta toward Cairo, just as the annual flood was rising. Sultan al-Kamil let them pass, then had the dykes behind them cut. The water surrounded the crusader camp and turned their road into a swamp. Starving and trapped, they surrendered Damietta in exchange for safe passage. The Nile itself had fought for Egypt.",
  "ayyubids:wind": "Imagined: in spring the khamsin blows out of the Western Desert into Cairo, a hot wind loaded with sand. The sky turns orange over the Citadel that Saladin built on the Muqattam hills. Mamluk guards wrap their faces, camels kneel with their backs to the wind, and in the madrasas the students cannot read for the darkness. The old say it will blow for fifty days, but tomorrow may be clear.",
  "ayyubids:fire": "During the siege of Acre in 1190, the crusaders built three great siege towers that no fire could burn. Ibn Shaddad tells how a young coppersmith from Damascus offered a mixture of naphtha and other substances he had prepared. He threw a pot at one tower: nothing. Then another, and it burst into flame, then the next. The Muslims on the walls shouted with joy; Saladin wanted to reward him, but he refused.",
  "ayyubids:thunder": "Imagined: a thunderstorm breaks over the Ayyubid camp outside Acre in winter. Tents collapse, horses scream, the trenches fill with water. Saladin, ill with fever, walks among the soldiers to see they are sheltered. Across the field the crusader camp suffers worse. Both armies hear the same thunder, and in both, men pray that the siege will end before they do.",
  "ayyubids:birth": "Saladin was born in Tikrit in 1137 or 1138, on the very night his father Ayyub and uncle Shirkuh were forced to flee the town after Shirkuh killed a man. Ayyub thought the child had brought bad luck and wanted nothing to do with him. One of his men answered: you do not know, this child may become a great and famous king. Fifty years later he took Jerusalem.",
  "ayyubids:death": "Saladin died in Damascus on 4 March 1193, after twelve days of fever. Ibn Shaddad wrote that the treasury held only forty-seven dirhams and one gold piece; he had given everything away, and money had to be borrowed for his funeral. He was washed and shrouded, prayed over, and buried that afternoon. There was no music, only the weeping of a city that felt it had lost a father.",

  // ---------------- Turks (Seljuks and older Turkic peoples) ----------------
  "turks:hunt": "Malik-Shah, the Seljuk sultan who ruled from Anatolia to Central Asia in the 1070s and 1080s, loved hunting above everything. Chroniclers say he counted his kills and gave a gold dinar to the poor for every animal he took, so that his pleasure would be paid for in charity. At Kufa he raised a tower studded with the horns and hooves of the gazelles he had shot.",
  "turks:battle": "On Friday 26 August 1071, at Manzikert, Sultan Alp Arslan prayed with his army, dressed in white, and said it would be his shroud if he fell. He tied up his horse's tail with his own hand. The Seljuk horse archers drew the Byzantine army forward, then closed around it. Emperor Romanos IV was captured, and Alp Arslan treated him with honour and let him go. Anatolia opened to the Turks.",
  "turks:gather": "Each spring the Turkmen clans of Anatolia moved their black goat-hair tents up to the yayla, the high summer pastures. Women milked ewes and made yogurt, cheese and butter, and wove the knotted carpets that would be sold in Konya. In autumn they came down again with wool, felt and dried curds. The yearly migration was the harvest, and the old Oghuz songs went up and down with it.",
  "turks:ritual": "The Byzantine historian Menander, writing of the Turks of the 560s, says they honoured fire, water and earth, but worshipped only the creator of heaven and earth, Tengri, sacrificing horses, cattle and sheep to him. Their shamans, kam, foretold the future. On the Orkhon stones the khagans say they ruled because Tengri willed it, and that when the people forgot him, they were lost.",
  "turks:rain": "The old Turkic peoples knew the yada tash, the rain stone. A yadachi, a rain-maker, would dip the stone in water and chant, calling clouds, snow or storm. Mahmud al-Kashgari in 1072 wrote that he had seen it himself: a man brought snow and rain in summer with such a stone and put out a fire. For herders on the dry steppe, it was the most precious magic of all.",
  "turks:flood": "Imagined: in spring the snow melts on the Taurus mountains and the river below the Turkmen village runs wild, brown and roaring. The flood carries off a mill and a flock of sheep, and the men stand on the bank with ropes, unable to do anything. The old woman says the river spirit is hungry this year. In the evening they cook a lamb and share it with the whole village, for luck.",
  "turks:wind": "Imagined: a dust storm crosses the plain of Konya in late summer. The caravan stops at the Sultan Han, the great stone caravanserai built by the Seljuk sultan Kayqubad in 1229. Camels are led through the carved gate, merchants crowd into the vaulted hall, and the iron doors are shut. Outside the wind screams for hours. Inside, someone tells stories of Dede Korkut until it passes.",
  "turks:fire": "In 569 the Byzantine envoy Zemarchus reached the court of the Turkic khagan Istemi in the Altai. Menander records that before he could enter, Turkic shamans beat drums and rang bells over his baggage, carried burning frankincense around him, and made him walk between two fires to purify him of evil. Fire, for the old Turks, cleansed whatever came from outside.",
  "turks:thunder": "Imagined: on the open steppe, a herder sees the storm before he hears it. The old Turks believed thunder was Tengri's voice and that lightning struck where something impure lay. When a tree or a tent was struck, the place was left alone and offerings were made. He brings his horses together, ties them, and waits on foot, small under the enormous sky, until the rain arrives.",
  "turks:birth": "Old Turkic mothers called on Umay, the goddess of birth and children. The Orkhon inscription of Kul Tigin says his mother was like Umay. In the yurt a woman in labour held a rope hung from the roof frame, while midwives and the women of the family helped, and the afterbirth was buried with care, since Umay was believed to guard it. Then the child was given a name, and the clan wondered what it would become.",
  "turks:death": "Chinese chronicles describe the Göktürk funeral. The body was laid in a tent, and relatives rode seven times around it, crying out and slashing their faces with knives so that blood ran with tears. On a chosen day the dead was burned with his horse and goods, and a stone statue raised over the grave, with a line of stones, balbals, one for each enemy he had killed. Kul Tigin was mourned this way in 731.",

  // ---------------- Ottomans ----------------
  "ottomans:hunt": "Sultan Mehmed IV, who reigned from 1648 to 1687, was so devoted to the chase that he was called Avci, the Hunter. From his palace at Edirne he rode out for weeks at a time, and villagers by the thousands were ordered to beat the forests, driving deer, boar and wolves toward him. Courtiers grumbled that he hunted while the empire's wars went badly; the sultan answered that the hunt kept him strong for the saddle.",
  "ottomans:battle": "Before dawn on 29 May 1453, Mehmed II launched the final assault on Constantinople. The mehter band played on behind the lines, kettledrums and zurnas sounding without pause so that the soldiers would not lose heart. Weeks earlier his ships had been dragged overland on greased logs into the Golden Horn. When the Janissaries broke through near the Gate of St Romanus, the city of a thousand years fell. The sultan was twenty-one.",
  "ottomans:gather": "Every June the silkworm cocoons of the Bursa countryside came in. Farmers carried them in sacks to the Koza Han, the Cocoon Inn built in 1491 by Bayezid II, where merchants from Persia, Italy and the whole empire bargained in the courtyard around the small mosque. The cocoons were boiled, the threads reeled, and the silk woven into the velvets and brocades that dressed the Ottoman court.",
  "ottomans:ritual": "When the sultan went to war, the Sancak-i Serif, the holy banner said to have belonged to the Prophet, was brought out of its chest in Topkapi Palace. The Quran was recited, the ulema prayed, and the sultan or grand vizier received the banner with tears and kissed it. It was carried at the head of the army. Muslims did not ask fortune-tellers about the campaign; they asked God, and accepted His answer.",
  "ottomans:rain": "In years of drought, the people of Istanbul climbed to the open-air prayer ground at Okmeydani, above the Golden Horn. Scholars, artisans, children and even animals were brought along, because Muslims believed God might show mercy for the sake of the innocent. They prayed the yagmur duasi, the prayer for rain, and the preachers urged everyone to repay debts and make peace. Evliya Celebi describes these crowds walking home hopeful.",
  "ottomans:flood": "In September 1563, a sudden flood swept down the valleys north of Istanbul, tearing through the Kagithane stream and destroying parts of the aqueducts that carried water to the city. Houses and mills were washed away, and many drowned. Suleiman the Magnificent ordered his architect Mimar Sinan to rebuild the Kirkcesme water system, and Sinan raised great stone aqueducts across the valleys, some still standing today.",
  "ottomans:wind": "In October 1541 the emperor Charles V landed a huge army outside Algiers, defended by Hasan Agha for the Ottoman sultan. Then a terrible storm blew in from the sea. Rain soaked the Spanish gunpowder, and over a hundred and fifty ships were driven onto the shore and wrecked. The defenders sallied out, and the emperor had to sail away. In Algiers the storm was remembered as God's help to the faithful.",
  "ottomans:fire": "Istanbul was a city of wooden houses, and its great fear was the cry yangin var, there is fire. In July 1660 a fire began near Eminonu and burned for days, destroying much of the city, mosques, markets and tens of thousands of homes. The mother of the sultan, Turhan Hatice, used the ruins to complete the New Mosque at the waterfront. Later the tulumbaci firefighters ran through the streets carrying pumps on their shoulders.",
  "ottomans:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm crashes over the Bosphorus. Lightning strikes the sea near Uskudar, and fishermen row hard for shore. In Topkapi, the pages of the palace school stop their calligraphy lesson and look out over the water. Their old teacher says nothing; he only finishes the letter he was drawing, then recites a prayer for those at sea. When the thunder fades, the lesson continues.",
  "ottomans:birth": "When a son was born to the sultan, cannons fired from Topkapi, and Istanbul was illuminated with lamps for days; the crowd cheered. But everyone knew what being a prince meant. Under the law of fratricide, when Mehmed III became sultan in 1595, his nineteen brothers were strangled. A prince's birth was the hope of the dynasty and also its fear, and his mother prayed both that he would rule and that he would live.",
  "ottomans:death": "Suleiman the Magnificent died in his tent during the siege of Szigetvar in September 1566. The grand vizier Sokollu Mehmed Pasha hid his death for weeks so the army would not lose heart, until the new sultan was secure. The body was carried home, washed and shrouded, and prayed over at the Suleymaniye Mosque, then laid in its tomb. As with every Muslim funeral there was no music, only recitation and prayer.",

  // ---------------- Armenians ----------------
  "armenians:hunt": "Movses Khorenatsi tells of King Artavazd, cursed by his dying father. One day while hunting on the slopes of Mount Masis, Ararat, he was seized by madness, his horse plunged into a chasm, and he vanished. Armenian villagers said he was chained in a cave, guarded by dogs who licked his chains thin, and that blacksmiths struck their anvils each Sunday to keep him bound. The hunt became a warning.",
  "armenians:battle": "On 26 May 451, at Avarayr, Vardan Mamikonian led the Armenians against the Sasanian Persians, who demanded they give up Christianity. The Armenians were defeated and Vardan fell with many companions. Yet the resistance went on until Persia granted freedom of religion in the treaty of Nvarsak in 484. The church honours them each year at Vardanants: a lost battle remembered as a victory of conscience.",
  "armenians:gather": "Armenians say Noah planted the first vineyard below Ararat when the ark came to rest. In the Areni-1 cave archaeologists found a winery more than 6,000 years old, with a pressing basin and storage jars. Each autumn the vintage still comes in: families cut grapes on the terraces, press them, and bless the first wine. At the feast of the Assumption the grapes are blessed in church before anyone eats them.",
  "armenians:ritual": "The Armenian Church keeps the matagh, an offering of a lamb or rooster on behalf of the sick, the dead, or someone setting out on a journey. A priest blesses salt, the animal is given salt to eat, and then it is slaughtered and the meat shared among the poor and neighbours. Nothing is burned on an altar; the offering is the meal shared, and the prayer that God will look kindly on what comes.",
  "armenians:rain": "In high summer, when the fields of the Ararat plain are parched, Armenians celebrate Vardavar. Once it honoured the goddess Astghik; now it is the feast of the Transfiguration. People throw buckets of water over one another in the streets, children ambush adults, and no one can be angry. It is laughter, but also an old wish that the sky will do the same, and that rain will come before the wheat is lost.",
  "armenians:wind": "In the winter of 401 BCE, Xenophon's Greek army crossed the Armenian highlands in a north wind so fierce that men lost toes to frostbite and snow lay deep. They sacrificed to the wind. Then they found Armenian villages with houses dug underground, where families lived beside their goats and cows and served barley beer drunk through straws. The villagers had learned long before how to wait out such storms.",
  "armenians:fire": "On Trndez, in February, Armenians light a great bonfire in the churchyard from candles blessed in church. Newlyweds jump over it hand in hand, then young people who hope to marry, then everyone else; women who want children walk around it. The ashes are scattered on the fields for fertility. Fire, here, means warmth returning after winter, and the promise of the coming spring.",
  "armenians:thunder": "In the old Armenian faith, Vahagn was the god of thunder and war, the dragon-slayer, Vishapakagh. When lightning split the summer sky over the mountains, it was Vahagn striking the vishaps, the dragons who swallowed rain and hid it in the clouds. Stone dragon-fish, vishapakar, still stand by highland springs. The thunder was frightening, but it meant the waters were being freed.",
  "armenians:birth": "Movses Khorenatsi preserved an ancient song once sung with a lyre: heaven and earth were in labour, the purple sea was in labour, and a little red reed in the sea. From the reed's stem came smoke, from it flame, and from the flame a little boy ran, with hair of fire and a beard of flame, and his eyes were suns. It was the birth of Vahagn, and of every Armenian hope.",
  "armenians:death": "After an Armenian death, the family keeps the body at home for a night with candles; women sing laments for the dead by name. The priest leads the requiem, the hogehangist, and the dead are buried facing east. Over the centuries Armenians carved khachkars, stone crosses laced like embroidery, for the salvation of a soul. At Noratus by Lake Sevan, nearly a thousand stand together in the grass.",

  // ---------------- Georgians ----------------
  "georgians:hunt": "Shota Rustaveli's Knight in the Panther's Skin, written around 1200 for Queen Tamar, opens with a hunt. King Rostevan of Arabia and his young commander Avtandil compete with bows across the plain, servants counting every kill, and the king laughs when the young man wins. Then they see a stranger weeping by a stream in a panther's skin, and the adventure begins. Georgians still learn its verses by heart.",
  "georgians:battle": "On 12 August 1121, at Didgori, King David IV the Builder met a far larger Seljuk coalition. He had reformed his army, and two hundred of his men rode forward as if to surrender, then struck the enemy commanders. Georgian cavalry charged from the hills, and the coalition broke. Georgians call it the miraculous victory, dzlevai sakvirveli. A year later David took Tbilisi back, and the golden age began.",
  "georgians:gather": "Every autumn is rtveli, the grape harvest. Whole villages in Kakheti go into the vineyards together; the grapes are trodden in a wooden trough, and the juice, skins and stems poured into a qvevri, a huge clay jar buried in the earth, sealed to ferment until spring. Afterwards comes the supra, a feast led by a tamada, toastmaster, and three-part songs that rise until late at night.",
  "georgians:ritual": "In the mountains of Khevsureti, each community had its khati, a shrine, served by a khevisberi, a priest chosen from the village elders. At the festival he brewed sacred beer, slaughtered sheep, and spoke to the shrine's divine patron for everyone. Sometimes a kadagi, a seer, fell into a trance and spoke the deity's will about the coming year. People listened in silence, men on one side, women at a distance.",
  "georgians:rain": "In Georgian villages, during a drought, children made a doll called Lazare or Gonja from a stick dressed in rags and carried it from house to house singing, Lazare, give us rain. Each family poured water over the doll and gave the children eggs, flour or cheese. Then the doll was thrown into a river. It was play, and also a real prayer by people whose bread depended on the sky.",
  "georgians:flood": "Imagined: in spring the Rioni river, the ancient Phasis of Colchis, swells with snowmelt from the Caucasus. The water spreads over the lowland fields and the farmers of Imereti watch from their wooden houses raised on posts. They pray to St George and wait. When the river withdraws it leaves fresh silt, and the maize will grow tall. The flood takes, and it gives.",
  "georgians:wind": "Imagined: a winter storm comes over the passes into Upper Svaneti. Snow drives sideways and wind howls through the gorges. Each Svan family climbs into its stone tower, built a thousand years ago for defence against enemies, avalanches and blood feuds. Up there, by a small fire, the grandfather sings a slow song with his sons in three voices, and the wind outside becomes part of the harmony.",
  "georgians:fire": "In September 1795 the Persian shah Agha Mohammad Khan defeated the small Georgian army at Krtsanisi and entered Tbilisi. The city was burned and plundered for days; churches were destroyed and thousands of people taken captive. King Erekle II, seventy-five years old, had to be dragged from the battlefield by his grandson. Georgians remember the three hundred Aragvians who fought to the last on the field.",
  "georgians:thunder": "In the mountains of Svaneti, lightning belonged to the prophet Elijah. When someone was struck and killed, the Svans did not mourn in the usual way, because the dead had been chosen by God. They danced slowly in a circle around the body, singing Elia lrde, an ancient hymn to Elijah, asking him to spare the living. Thunder in the high valleys was holy, terrible and close.",
  "georgians:birth": "In the high Caucasus, Khevsur and Pshav women gave birth in a small separate hut, the samrevlo, outside the village, because birth was considered impure for the shrine. They often laboured alone or with one helper. It was dangerous, and many died. Yet when a child came home, the family toasted it at the supra: may you be a good Georgian, may you be the one who brings our people luck.",
  "georgians:death": "When someone dies in Svaneti, the village gathers for the zari, the funeral chant: men sing in three voices a slow, harsh cry of ah and vai, almost without words, a sound some think older than Christianity. Women sit by the body and lament its name. After the burial comes the kelekhi, a memorial feast, with toasts to the dead and to the ancestors who receive them.",

  // ---------------- Huns ----------------
  "huns:hunt": "Jordanes tells an old story of how the Huns came west. Hunters on the far side of the Maeotic marshes, the Sea of Azov, followed a hind that kept stopping and looking back. She led them through shallows no one had known could be crossed, and then vanished. On the other side lay Scythia, rich and open. The hunters rode home, called their people, and a new age of the steppe began.",
  "huns:battle": "In 452 Attila besieged Aquileia in northern Italy for a long time, and his men began to grumble and want to leave. Then, Priscus and Jordanes say, Attila saw storks that nested on the city's roofs carrying their young out into the countryside. He told his army: look, the birds know this city will fall. The Huns took heart, brought up their siege engines, and Aquileia was taken and destroyed.",
  "huns:gather": "In 449 the Roman envoy Priscus rode through Hunnic lands to Attila's court. In the villages, he wrote, people gave them millet instead of wheat, and a drink they called medos, mead, while the servants drank kamon, made from barley. At Attila's wooden hall, guests were served on silver, but Attila himself ate meat from a wooden plate and wore plain clothes. Gathering and sharing was how power was shown.",
  "huns:ritual": "Before the battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451, Jordanes writes, Attila ordered his soothsayers to read the future in the entrails of cattle and in the streaks on scraped bones. They foretold disaster for the Huns, but said a great enemy leader would fall. Attila decided the price was worth paying. He fought, and Theodoric, king of the Visigoths, was killed. The prophecy had told the truth both ways.",
  "huns:rain": "Imagined: a hot, dry summer on the Pannonian plain. The grass is brown, the horses thin, and the herds must move to find water. Hunnic families pack their wagons and drive the animals toward the Tisza river. At night they watch the western sky for lightning. An old woman ties a strip of cloth to a tree and whispers to the sky that the children need the rain.",
  "huns:wind": "Imagined: a steppe storm rolls in from the east. A wall of dust darkens the sun, and the Hunnic camp moves fast: wagons are drawn into a circle, felt covers lashed down, horses turned with their tails to the wind. The riders cover their faces and wait. They are not afraid; the wind has always come with them, and the Romans say the Huns themselves came like a storm.",
  "huns:fire": "After the battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451, Attila withdrew into his camp behind a ring of wagons. Jordanes says he ordered a pyre built of horse saddles, so that if the Romans broke in, he would throw himself into the flames rather than be captured or killed by an enemy. The fire was never lit; the Romans and Goths let him go. But the saddles stood ready all night.",
  "huns:thunder": "Imagined: on the great plain a thunderstorm breaks over the Hunnic camp. The horses stamp and scream, and lightning splits a lone tree near the river. The shamans say the sky god is angry and must be calmed with a horse sacrifice. Children hide under the wagons, counting the flashes. By morning the grass is green again, and the storm is told around the fire as a story of power.",
  "huns:birth": "Priscus, at Attila's banquet in 449, noticed that the king, stern and unsmiling with everyone else, drew his youngest son Ernak close and looked at him with gentle eyes, pinching his cheek. A Hun explained: the soothsayers had told Attila that his family would fall, but would be restored by this boy. Every father hopes his child will save the house; Attila had been told so, and believed it.",
  "huns:death": "Attila died in 453 on the night of his wedding. Jordanes, following Priscus, says his body lay in a silk tent in the middle of the plain while the best horsemen rode around it singing a funeral song, the strava, of his deeds. His men cut off their hair and gashed their faces, so that the greatest warrior was mourned not with tears but with blood. He was buried secretly in three coffins of gold, silver and iron.",

  // ---------------- Cumans (Kipchaks) ----------------
  "cumans:hunt": "Imagined: a Cuman khan rides out on the Pontic steppe with his falconers and a golden eagle on a leather glove. Dogs flush a fox from the feather-grass, the eagle drops on it from a great height, and the men cheer. Hunting kept the young riders sharp for war and filled the pots in winter. Behind them, on a kurgan, a stone statue of an ancestor watches the plain.",
  "cumans:battle": "In 1185 Prince Igor of Novgorod-Seversk rode against the Cumans, the Polovtsians, to win glory. The Cuman khans Konchak and Gzak gathered their warriors and surrounded his army at the river Kayala. After three days of fighting without water, the Rus were destroyed and Igor taken captive. Konchak treated him as a guest and later married his daughter to Igor's son. The Lay of Igor's Campaign mourns the defeat; the Cumans celebrated.",
  "cumans:gather": "Imagined: in early summer, the Cuman women milk the mares in the camp. The foals are tied in a line so the mares stay near, and the milk is poured into a leather bag and churned with a wooden plunger for days until it becomes kumis, sour and lightly alcoholic. A good season means bags full of kumis, fat sheep and many visitors, and songs late into the short nights.",
  "cumans:ritual": "In 1239 the Cumans sealed an alliance with the Latin emperor Baldwin II. Jean de Joinville recorded the ritual: the Cuman chiefs and Baldwin's men let blood from their hands into a cup, mixed it with wine and water, and drank it, swearing they were now brothers. Then a dog was cut in two between them, and they swore that whoever broke the oath would be cut down the same way.",
  "cumans:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer the Cuman clans gather beside a stone statue on a kurgan, the image of an ancestor holding a cup at his belly. They pour kumis over its feet, place meat before it, and ask the ancestors to speak to Tengri for rain. The elders watch the sky to the west. If clouds come in three days, the offering was accepted.",
  "cumans:wind": "Imagined: a winter buran sweeps across the Pontic steppe, driving snow so thick that a rider cannot see his horse's head. The Cuman herds turn their backs to it and drift with the wind; the herders follow, roped together, singing to keep the animals calm. A family may lose half its sheep in one night. When it passes, everyone counts what is left, and goes on.",
  "cumans:fire": "In 1239, fleeing the Mongols, Khan Koten led some forty thousand Cuman families into Hungary. In 1241 Hungarian nobles, suspecting them of spying for the Mongols, murdered Koten in Pest. Enraged, the Cumans left the kingdom, burning villages behind them as they went south into Bulgaria. Smoke rose along their path. They felt betrayed by the people who had promised them refuge.",
  "cumans:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm builds over the steppe near the Dnieper. Lightning stabs down, and a Cuman herder throws himself flat beside his horse. The Cumans believed Tengri spoke in thunder, and that a place struck by lightning was touched by heaven. Afterwards the family will bring milk to the scorched grass and leave it there, with a quiet word of respect.",
  "cumans:birth": "In 1262 Elizabeth the Cuman, daughter of a Cuman chief, gave birth to Ladislaus, heir to the Hungarian throne. The Cumans who had settled in Hungary hoped he would be their protector. He was: as king, he dressed like a Cuman, lived among them and loved a Cuman girl, until he was murdered by Cuman assassins in 1290. A mother's hope and a people's hope, held in one child.",
  "cumans:death": "Jean de Joinville describes the burial of a Cuman knight in the 1240s, near Constantinople. A large pit was dug and the dead man set inside, sitting upright in fine clothes. With him they placed his best horse and his squire, both alive, and gold and silver. The khan gave great gifts so that the dead would arrive in the next world as a rich man. Then they covered the pit and raised a mound above it.",

  // ---------------- Tatars ----------------
  "tatars:hunt": "Imagined: on a frosty morning in the Crimean steppe, a Tatar bey rides out with his hounds and a goshawk. Hares break from the dry grass and the hawk strikes. The men gallop and shout, and the bey's young son brings down his first hare with a bow. Tonight there will be a small feast, and the boy's grandfather will tell him he rides like his ancestors.",
  "tatars:battle": "In October 1552 Ivan IV of Moscow besieged Kazan, capital of the Tatar khanate. For weeks the defenders under Khan Yadegar Mokhammad held the walls against cannon fire. On 2 October Russian sappers blew mines under the walls and stormed the city. The mosques were destroyed and many of the defenders killed in the streets. Tatar songs still remember the fall of Kazan and the Kul Sharif mosque.",
  "tatars:gather": "Sabantuy, the festival of the plough, marks the end of spring sowing among the Volga Tatars. Young men ride from house to house collecting gifts: embroidered towels, scarves, eggs. Then the whole village gathers on a meadow for wrestling with belts, horse races, sack races and climbing a greased pole for a prize. The work is done, and everyone hopes the fields will repay it.",
  "tatars:ritual": "Imagined: in a Kazan Tatar village, a young man must decide whether to travel to Bukhara to study. He does not go to a fortune-teller. He washes, prays two units of prayer at night, and recites the istikhara supplication: O God, if this is good for my faith and my life, make it easy for me. In the morning he tells his mother he will go, and she begins to pack.",
  "tatars:rain": "In dry summers Tatar villages held yangyr televe, asking for rain. People gathered by a river or on a hill, cooked porridge together in a great cauldron, shared it with everyone, and prayed. Then they splashed water on each other, and sometimes even threw the mullah gently into the river. Everyone laughed, but they looked at the sky all the same, hoping the prayer had been heard.",
  "tatars:wind": "Imagined: a winter buran falls on the Volga steppe. In the Tatar village the wind fills the roads with snow up to the windows. Families bring the calves inside, and the grandmother lights the lamp and reads aloud from an old book of stories while the house creaks. In the morning the men dig tunnels to the mosque, and the muezzin calls the dawn prayer as usual.",
  "tatars:fire": "In May 1571 the Crimean Tatar khan Devlet Giray raided all the way to Moscow. On the day his army arrived, a fire began in the suburbs and, driven by wind, swept through the wooden city. The Kremlin's powder magazine exploded. Chroniclers said tens of thousands died. For the Crimean Tatars it was revenge for Kazan and Astrakhan; for Moscow, a catastrophe it never forgot.",
  "tatars:thunder": "In 922 Ibn Fadlan travelled from Baghdad to the Volga Bulgars, ancestors of the Kazan Tatars. He wrote that when lightning struck a house, the Bulgars would not go near it, but left it with everything inside to perish, saying it was a house on which wrath had fallen. He also saw the northern lights, and his hosts told him they were believing and unbelieving jinn fighting in the sky.",
  "tatars:birth": "When a Tatar baby is born, family and neighbours gather for isem kushu, the naming. The mullah takes the child, faces Mecca, and recites the call to prayer into the right ear and the iqama into the left. Then he whispers the name three times. Guests bring gifts and the mother rests for forty days. Everyone prays the child will be healthy, learned and honest, whatever times it lives in.",
  "tatars:death": "Tatar funerals follow Islamic law and have no music. The body is washed, wrapped in a white shroud, and buried the same day if possible, carried on men's shoulders while they recite prayers. Only men go to the graveyard. At home, the women read Yasin, and on the third, seventh and fortieth days the family gathers for prayers and a meal, remembering the dead and asking forgiveness for them.",

  // ---------------- Golden Horde ----------------
  "golden-horde:hunt": "The Mongols' great hunt, the nerge, was a war exercise. Juvaini describes how the whole army formed a ring many days' ride across and slowly drew it tighter, driving every animal inside toward the centre. No beast could escape and no man could break the line without punishment. At last the khan rode in first to shoot, then the princes, then the soldiers. The Golden Horde khans kept the custom on the Volga steppe.",
  "golden-horde:battle": "On 11 April 1241, at Mohi on the Sajo river, Batu Khan and Subutai met the army of King Bela IV of Hungary. Batu's men forced the bridge with catapults, while Subutai crossed downstream in secret. The Hungarians, trapped in their wagon camp, were cut down as they fled. Batu mourned Bahadur, one of his commanders, killed at the bridge. Within weeks the Horde stood on the Danube.",
  "golden-horde:gather": "Ibn Battuta visited the camp of Khan Ozbeg in 1334 and wrote that it was a moving city: wagons with felt houses on top, mosques and markets travelling with it, smoke rising from kitchens as it rolled. At Sarai on the Volga, merchants from Egypt, Genoa, Persia and Russia gathered. In the camp, the khatuns hosted feasts with kumis served in gold and silver cups.",
  "golden-horde:ritual": "William of Rubruck, a friar sent to the Mongols in 1253, describes how the khans asked about the future. A sheep's shoulder blade was placed in the fire until it cracked from the heat. Then it was brought to the khan, who read the cracks: if they ran straight, the plan would succeed; if crooked, it would fail. No great undertaking began without it, before the khans of the Horde turned to Islam.",
  "golden-horde:rain": "Imagined: in a dry year on the Volga steppe, the grass is short and the herds thin. In Sarai, Khan Ozbeg's people go out after Friday prayer for the istisqa, the prayer for rain. Out in the grasslands, the herding families still pour milk to the sky as their grandparents did. Both wait together, Muslim and old believer, for the clouds that will decide the winter.",
  "golden-horde:wind": "Imagined: a buran strikes the Horde's winter camp on the lower Volga. Snow and wind blow for three days. The felt tents are weighted with stones, and the wagons drawn into a ring around them. Herders work in shifts to keep the horses moving so they do not freeze. When the wind falls, the khan sends riders to every camp to count the losses and share food.",
  "golden-horde:fire": "When John of Plano Carpini, envoy of the Pope, reached Batu's camp in 1246, he was told he must pass between two fires before seeing the khan, along with his gifts. The Mongols believed fire cleansed visitors of any poison or evil they might carry. Two spears stood beside the fires with a rope between them, hung with strips of cloth. Then he was led into Batu's golden tent.",
  "golden-horde:thunder": "Plano Carpini and Rubruck both noticed that the Mongols, who feared nothing in battle, were terribly afraid of thunder. During a storm they drove strangers out of their tents, wrapped themselves in black felt, and hid until it passed. If someone was struck by lightning, his family had to pass between fires to be purified, and nobody touched the things in his tent.",
  "golden-horde:birth": "Jochi, the eldest son of Genghis Khan and father of Batu, was born around 1182, soon after his mother Borte was rescued from the Merkits who had captured her. Some whispered he was not Genghis's son. Genghis accepted him, but his brother Chagatai once called him a Merkit bastard in front of their father. Jochi's line went on to found the Golden Horde, the longest-lasting of the Mongol khanates.",
  "golden-horde:death": "Plano Carpini describes the burial of a Mongol noble. He was placed in a tent seated at a table, with a bowl of meat and a cup of mare's milk, and buried with a mare and her foal and a saddled horse, so that he would have milk and a mount in the next world. Another horse was eaten, and its skin stuffed and raised on poles over the grave. Then the place was hidden.",

  // ---------------- Berbers (Amazigh, Tuareg) ----------------
  "berbers:hunt": "On the rock walls of Tassili n'Ajjer, deep in today's Sahara, painters thousands of years ago drew hunters with bows chasing giraffes and antelope across what was then green savanna. Tuareg herders still camp beneath those paintings. Imagined: a Tuareg hunter tracks a Barbary sheep among the rocks of the Hoggar at dawn, moving barefoot and silent, as his grandfather taught him, knowing every animal killed must be shared.",
  "berbers:battle": "Around 698, the Amazigh queen Dihya, whom the Arabs called al-Kahina, the prophetess, led the tribes of the Aures mountains against the Umayyad general Hassan ibn al-Nu'man. At the river Meskiana she routed his army and drove it out of Ifriqiya. She treated the captives well and even adopted one of them, Khalid, as her son. For five years the Aures remained free.",
  "berbers:gather": "In the southwest of Morocco, Amazigh women gather the fallen fruit of the argan tree, found almost nowhere else on earth. Goats climb into the branches to eat the leaves. The women sit together, cracking each hard nut between two stones, one by one, talking and singing, then roast and grind the kernels by hand to press out the oil. A day's work makes barely a litre.",
  "berbers:ritual": "Herodotus wrote that the Nasamones of Libya, ancestors of Amazigh peoples, asked their dead for guidance by sleeping on the tombs of their forefathers and taking the dream as an answer. Centuries later, as Muslims, Amazigh villagers bring their questions to God in istikhara prayer and to the shrines of local saints, but the old respect for the ancestors who lie under the stones still runs deep.",
  "berbers:rain": "In dry years, Amazigh villages carried out the taghonja: girls dressed a large wooden ladle as a bride and carried it from house to house singing to Anzar, the rain. The old legend says Anzar loved a girl and wanted her as his bride; when she agreed, the rain came back to the fields. Women poured water over the doll, and everyone watched the Atlas peaks for clouds.",
  "berbers:wind": "In the 19th century travellers reported that in 1805 a great caravan crossing from Timbuktu toward the salt mines of the north, two thousand people and nearly as many camels, was caught by the simoom, the poison wind, and the wells were dry. Not one survived. Tuareg guides, the masters of that desert, read the sky and the sand with care because they knew how quickly the wind could kill.",
  "berbers:fire": "Arab chroniclers tell that when Dihya, al-Kahina, knew the Umayyad armies would return, she ordered her people to burn the towns and cut down the orchards from Tripoli to Tangier, so that invaders would find nothing worth taking. Smoke rose over the olive groves. Many of her own people turned against her for it. She fought on, and fell around 703, at a well later called Bir al-Kahina.",
  "berbers:thunder": "Imagined: in the High Atlas a summer storm breaks over a village of red earth houses. Thunder echoes from cliff to cliff, and the women hurry to bring in the barley drying on the rooftops. The children count the flashes. The old man at the mosque door says the storm is a mercy as long as it does not bring hail, and murmurs a prayer for the terraces below.",
  "berbers:birth": "Imagined: in a Tuareg camp a young woman gives birth in her mother's tent, as custom requires for a first child. The women surround her, singing softly, and the midwife holds her hands. On the seventh day comes the naming: a ram is slaughtered, the marabout whispers the name into the baby's ear, and the camp celebrates with tende drumming and camel races. No one knows what this child will see.",
  "berbers:death": "In a Tuareg legend, Tin Hinan was the queen and ancestress of all the Tuareg. At Abalessa in the Hoggar archaeologists found a tomb from about the 4th century where a woman lay on a carved bed, wearing gold and silver bracelets. Today Amazigh funerals follow Islam: the dead are washed, shrouded and buried the same day with prayers, and there is no music, only silence and the Quran.",

  // ---------------- Ethiopians ----------------
  "ethiopians:hunt": "Imagined: in the highlands near Gondar in the 17th century, an Ethiopian noble rides out with his men and their spears after a buffalo that has been raiding the fields. The hunters move through the tall grass, calling to each other in short, sharp shouts. When the buffalo charges, a young man stands his ground. That night the village sings his praises, and his name will be remembered in fukera, the boasting songs.",
  "ethiopians:battle": "On 1 March 1896, at Adwa, the army of Emperor Menelik II and Empress Taytu Betul destroyed an invading Italian force. Some hundred thousand Ethiopians, marching from every province, had answered Menelik's call. The Italians lost thousands, and Ethiopia remained independent while nearly all of Africa was colonised. Adwa is still celebrated each year as a victory for every African people.",
  "ethiopians:gather": "Ethiopians tell of Kaldi, a goatherd of Kaffa who saw his goats dancing after eating red berries from a shrub. Coffee, buna, still grows wild in those forests. At the coffee ceremony a woman roasts the green beans over coals, carries the smoking pan around so everyone can smell it, grinds them by hand, and serves three rounds: abol, tona and baraka, the last one a blessing.",
  "ethiopians:ritual": "On the eve of Timkat, Epiphany, priests carry the tabot, the sacred tablet of each church, wrapped in brocade, to a pool or stream. Crowds in white keep vigil through the night. At dawn the water is blessed and sprinkled over everyone. Then priests in embroidered robes sway and chant, shaking the tsenatsil sistrum to the slow beat of the kebero drum. It is prayer as a whole people, asking blessing for the year.",
  "ethiopians:rain": "In the Ethiopian highlands, everything waits for kiremt, the great rains from June to September. When they come, the roads turn to mud, rivers rise, and the hills turn green. Farmers plough with oxen and sow teff. If the rains are late, priests lead processions and the people pray. In the famine years of 1888 to 1892 the rains failed; Ethiopians still call it Kifu Qen, the evil days.",
  "ethiopians:flood": "The Blue Nile rises from Lake Tana and carries most of the summer flood that once made Egypt fertile. Ethiopian rulers knew it. Several medieval Ethiopian kings were said to have threatened the sultans of Cairo that they could divert the Nile if Christians in Egypt were mistreated. Whether it was ever possible, the flood that Egypt celebrated began as Ethiopian rain on Ethiopian hills.",
  "ethiopians:wind": "Imagined: in the Danakil Depression, one of the hottest places on earth, an Afar salt caravan of a hundred camels is caught by a dust storm. The men cover their faces with cloth and make the camels kneel in a circle. The salt slabs, cut by hand from the salt flats, are tied tight. For hours there is only sand and heat. Then the wind dies, and they march on toward the highlands.",
  "ethiopians:fire": "On Meskel, 27 September, Ethiopians build a tall pyramid of branches tied with yellow Meskel daisies, the demera. It recalls the story of Empress Helena, who lit a fire and followed its smoke to find the buried True Cross. At dusk the demera is lit amid singing and dancing, and when it falls, people read the direction of the collapse as a sign for the coming year. The rains are over; the flowers are out.",
  "ethiopians:thunder": "Imagined: a kiremt storm breaks over the highlands of Gojjam. Lightning plays on the round thatched roofs and the thunder rolls down the gorge of the Blue Nile. In a round church on a hill, the debtera keep chanting the night prayers by candlelight, their voices low and steady. The village children listen through the walls and feel safe, because the chant does not stop for the storm.",
  "ethiopians:birth": "In the 12th century, a son was born to the Zagwe royal family at Roha. Tradition says a swarm of bees surrounded the baby without stinging him, and his mother took it as a sign of kingship, naming him Lalibela, the bees recognise his sovereignty. His brother feared him and tried to poison him. Lalibela lived, became king, and had eleven churches carved from solid rock, a new Jerusalem in Ethiopia.",
  "ethiopians:death": "When an Ethiopian dies, the wailing begins at once; neighbours come running. Women mourners, the alqash, improvise laments praising the dead, sometimes beating their chests. The priest leads the funeral at the church and the body is buried in the churchyard. Then come days of lekso, mourning, when visitors sit with the family in a tent. On the fortieth day, the tazkar, a memorial feast is held.",

  // ---------------- Malians (Mali Empire, Mande) ----------------
  "malians:hunt": "Among the Mande, hunters belong to the donso ton, a brotherhood with its own secrets, protective amulets and its own musicians, who play the donso ngoni harp and sing the deeds of great hunters. The epic of Sundiata begins with them: it was a hunter-seer who came to the king of Manden and told him to marry an ugly, hunchbacked woman, Sogolon, for she would bear him a mighty son.",
  "malians:battle": "Around 1235, at Kirina, Sundiata Keita met Sumanguru Kante, the sorcerer-king of Sosso, who had conquered Manden. The griots say no iron could harm Sumanguru, until Sundiata's sister Nana Triban, who had been given to Sumanguru as a wife, learned his secret: a white rooster's spur. Sundiata's arrow, tipped with it, struck him, and his power broke. The Mali Empire was born on that field.",
  "malians:gather": "The wealth of Mali came from the gold fields of Bambuk and Bure, near the upper Niger. In the dry season, farmers turned miners dug narrow pits and women washed the earth in calabashes, swirling until the gold grains shone. Mali's kings let the people keep the gold dust but claimed every nugget. Arab writers marvelled; on his hajj Mansa Musa spent so much gold that its price in Cairo fell for years.",
  "malians:ritual": "At Kangaba, every seven years, the Kamablon, the sacred round house of the Keita, gets a new thatched roof. Men of the village climb up and replace the old straw in one day while crowds watch. Then griots of the Diabate family from Kela, who keep the oral history, recite the story of Sundiata and the founding of Mali inside the house, a ceremony said to go back to the 14th century.",
  "malians:rain": "Imagined: in the Sahel near Niani the land has been dry for seven months, fields cracked and grey. At the end of May the first wind brings the smell of rain. People stand outside their compounds watching the clouds build over the south. When the first heavy drops fall, children run naked into it, laughing, and the old farmers kneel to touch the earth, ready to sow millet.",
  "malians:flood": "Each year the Niger spreads across its inland delta like a sea. When the water falls, Fulani herders bring their cattle back across the river at Diafarabe in the Dewgal, the great crossing. Herders swim with their herds through the current, and the whole town gathers to watch. The best-fed herds are praised in song, and the young men who brought them home are honoured. The river gives life twice a year.",
  "malians:wind": "Imagined: in December the harmattan blows south from the Sahara over Timbuktu. Fine dust hangs in the air, and the sun becomes a pale disc. In the Sankore mosque, scholars wrap their manuscripts in leather and close the wooden chests. Students cover their mouths and keep reciting. For three months the wind dries everything, but the cold nights are clear, and the stars are sharp over the dunes.",
  "malians:fire": "In January 2013, as armed groups retreated from Timbuktu, they set fire to the Ahmed Baba Institute, burning some of the old manuscripts kept there. But librarians, led by Abdel Kader Haidara, had already secretly smuggled hundreds of thousands of manuscripts out of the city in rice sacks and trunks, on donkey carts and river boats, to Bamako. The fire took pages; the people saved centuries.",
  "malians:thunder": "Imagined: a storm breaks over a Mande village at the start of the rains. Thunder rolls and lightning strikes a tall baobab at the edge of the fields. The blacksmith, whose craft is believed to command hidden powers, goes out with the elders to look at the tree. They say the storm has chosen it, and no one will cut it. The children whisper that the sky has marked their village.",
  "malians:birth": "The griots tell that Sogolon gave birth to Sundiata in a storm, and that the king's other wife, Sassouma, mocked the child, who could not walk until he was seven years old. Then one day, his mother humiliated by Sassouma, Sundiata called for an iron bar, hauled himself upright on it until the bar bent like a bow, and stood. The griots sing that the whole of Manden was born with him.",
  "malians:death": "Mande Muslims bury their dead quickly, the same day if they can. The body is washed, wrapped in white, and carried to the grave while men recite prayers; there is no music at the burial. Women mourn at home. In the days after, relatives come to greet the family, and in many families a griot, jeli, recalls the ancestors of the dead, so that the name and the lineage are not forgotten.",

  // ---------------- Hausa ----------------
  "hausa:hunt": "Imagined: in the dry season after the harvest, the hunters of a Hausa village gather under their leader, the sarkin baka, master of the bow. They carry bows, spears and charms and set out with dogs into the bush, beating it in a long line to drive out antelope and guinea fowl. At night they sing the songs of hunters by the fire, and the meat is shared with every compound.",
  "hausa:battle": "In the 16th century, says the Kano Chronicle, Amina, queen of Zazzau, led the armies of Zaria for decades, conquering lands as far as Nupe and Kwararafa, and the rulers paid her tribute. She is remembered for the earthen walls, ganuwar Amina, raised around her war camps and the towns of Hausaland. Hausa girls are still told: be like Amina, daughter of Bakwa Turunku.",
  "hausa:gather": "At Kofar Mata in Kano, the dye pits have been worked since 1498, by the city's own account. Dyers stir cloth into deep pits of indigo, fermented with ash and potash, and lift it out again and again until it is almost black. Then it is beaten with wooden mallets until it shines. Traders from across the Sahara came for Kano cloth, and the dyers' families still work the same pits.",
  "hausa:ritual": "At the end of Ramadan and at the Feast of Sacrifice, after the Eid prayer, the emirs of Kano, Katsina and Zaria hold the durbar. Horsemen in quilted armour and bright robes charge in groups toward the emir, reining in at the last moment to salute him, while kakaki trumpets blow and drums beat. It began as a review of the cavalry; it is now a prayer of thanks and a promise of loyalty.",
  "hausa:rain": "Imagined: in May the farmers of Katsina look to the sky every evening. The ground has been hard for months. When the first storm is seen in the south, the imam leads the prayer for rain and people give alms. Then the clouds come, and with them a rush to the fields: every hand, women and children too, plants millet and sorghum before the soil dries again.",
  "hausa:flood": "Imagined: in a year of heavy rains the Hadejia river breaks over its banks and spreads into the wetlands. Houses of mud brick soften and collapse; families carry what they can to higher ground. The village head organises shelter in the mosque, and neighbours from dry villages bring grain. When the water goes down, the land is rich again, and the rice will grow well next year.",
  "hausa:wind": "Imagined: the harmattan arrives in Kano in November, and soon the dust is so thick that the minarets disappear. Traders cover their goods in Kurmi market with mats, camels kneel by the city gates, and children wrap their faces in their mothers' wrappers. For months the cold dry wind cracks lips and skin. Then one day in February it stops, and the sky is suddenly blue.",
  "hausa:fire": "Imagined: in the dry season, when the harmattan blows, a cooking fire in a Hausa town catches a thatched roof. The flames jump from compound to compound. Men form lines from the wells with clay pots, women carry the children out, and the emir's servants pull down roofs to make a break. By evening a whole ward is ash. The next day neighbours come with grain, mats and cloth.",
  "hausa:thunder": "Imagined: the first great storm of the rainy season comes to a Hausa village at night. Thunder shakes the clay walls and lightning lights the granaries like day. The old women say the spirits of the old bori religion are dancing in the sky; the young imam says it is God's power. Everyone agrees on one thing: the rain is good, and tomorrow the planting begins.",
  "hausa:birth": "When a Hausa baby is born, the mother rests and is cared for in her own family's house, and visitors come with gifts. On the seventh day comes the suna, the naming: a ram is slaughtered at dawn, the imam whispers the name into the baby's ear, and friends crowd the compound with food and drumming. Everyone prays the child will be blessed, learned, and a credit to the family.",
  "hausa:death": "A Hausa Muslim funeral is simple and quick, and has no music. The body is washed, wrapped in white cloth and carried to the cemetery on a bier, usually the same day, while men recite prayers. Women stay at home. For days the family receives ta'aziyya, condolence visits, where friends sit with them and say: may God have mercy on the dead, may He give you patience.",
});
